{
  "rationale": "The binding constraints are the burst deadline and the accuracy floor. A 1 MHz tick dumps 32 requests at once and the last result must be back within 0.44 us, i.e. the unit must drain a 32-deep burst in ~32 service intervals plus pipeline latency. Only the fully-pipelined families (pipelined, pipelined_m) sustain 1 result/cycle, so 32 results cost ~32 cycles + latency, which is comfortably inside 0.44 us at any realistic Artix-7 clock (>= ~80 MHz suffices). iterative (N+3 cycles/result) and unrolled_k (ceil(N/k)+3 cycles/result) cannot drain the burst in time at achievable Fmax (e.g. unrolled_k k=8,N=8 still needs ~124 cycles for the last of 32 results), so they are excluded rather than wasting budget. Accuracy is identical across families and depends only on data_width/n_iter/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding, so I sweep those around the 2^-12 boundary (W~13-16, N~13-15) and out to high accuracy to map the whole luts+ffs vs accuracy_bits front. Because the objective is min luts+ffs, pipelined_m is important: registering every m stages cuts pipeline FFs (area) at the cost of Fmax, and Fmax only has to stay above the ~80 MHz the batch deadline needs, so large m may be area-optimal. Round 1 is a broad coarse sweep of both families; later rounds will refine the feasible boundary and the m/Fmax trade-off.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "Baseline 1 result/cycle family with one micro-rotation per pipeline stage, giving the shortest combinational path and highest Fmax, hence the most margin on the 0.44 us batch deadline. Sweep W and N across the 2^-12 accuracy boundary (and beyond, up to ~20 bits) to locate the cheapest feasible point and the low-area end of the front; angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding are swept because they shift the accuracy/cost boundary at fixed W,N.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "family": "pipelined",
      "budget_share": 0.45
    },
    {
      "why": "Same 1 result/cycle throughput and identical accuracy model, but registers every m stages, so pipeline FF count drops roughly by a factor m while the combinational path (and thus Fmax) grows. Since the area objective is min luts+ffs and the batch deadline only needs Fmax above ~80 MHz, this family can dominate the low-area end of the front. Sweep m 2..8 together with W/N/guards/rounding to find the area-optimal design that still meets the burst deadline, and to expose the Fmax-limited boundary where large m stops being viable.",
